# More starter Questions & Answers.
FAQ = [
('Advent Wreath', 'What does the Advent wreath mean?',
 'The evergreen circle stands for God’s everlasting love. Its four candles, three violet and one rose, are lit one by one on the Sundays of Advent, as the light grows brighter while we wait for Christ, the Light of the world. The rose candle is lit on Gaudete Sunday.',
 'John 8:12'),
('Almsgiving', 'What is almsgiving?',
 'Almsgiving is giving to the poor out of love for God and neighbour. With prayer and fasting, it is one of the three traditional practices of penance, especially in Lent. Jesus taught us to give quietly, without seeking praise.',
 'CCC 1434, 2447; Matthew 6:2–4'),
('Altar', 'Why is the altar so important in a Catholic church?',
 'The altar is the place where the sacrifice of the cross is made present in the Mass, and the table of the Lord to which the people are invited. It is a symbol of Christ himself, so the priest kisses it at the start and end of Mass.',
 'CCC 1182, 1383'),
('Beatitudes', 'What are the Beatitudes?',
 'They are the eight blessings Jesus taught at the start of the Sermon on the Mount, beginning “Blessed are the poor in spirit, for theirs is the kingdom of heaven.” They describe the heart of a disciple and the happiness God promises.',
 'CCC 1716–1724; Matthew 5:3–12'),
('Bible', 'Is the Bible without error?',
 'The Church teaches that, since God is the author of Scripture through inspired human writers, the books of the Bible teach firmly, faithfully and without error the truth God wanted put into writing for the sake of our salvation.',
 'CCC 105–108; Dei Verbum 11'),
('Candles', 'Why do Catholics light candles in church?',
 'A candle is a sign of prayer rising to God and of Christ, the Light of the world. Lighting a votive candle before a statue or icon is a way of continuing our prayer after we leave. Candles are also used at Mass, Baptism and funerals.',
 'John 8:12; Matthew 5:14–16'),
('Charity', 'What is charity?',
 'Charity is the theological virtue by which we love God above all things for his own sake, and our neighbour as ourselves for the love of God. It is the greatest of the virtues and the soul of every other.',
 'CCC 1822–1829; 1 Corinthians 13'),
('Chastity', 'What does the Church teach about chastity?',
 'Chastity is the virtue that integrates our sexuality within the whole person, so that we can love truly. Everyone is called to chastity according to their state of life: married couples by faithfulness, single people by continence. It is a source of freedom and joy.',
 'CCC 2337–2359'),
('Church', 'Can someone be saved outside the Catholic Church?',
 'The Church teaches that all salvation comes from Christ through his Church. Those who, through no fault of their own, do not know Christ or his Church, but seek God sincerely and try to do his will as their conscience tells them, may also attain eternal salvation.',
 'CCC 846–848; Lumen Gentium 16'),
('Communion', 'Can non-Catholics receive Holy Communion?',
 'Receiving Communion is a sign of full unity in faith with the Catholic Church, so normally only Catholics in a state of grace receive. Other Christians are warmly welcome at Mass and may come forward for a blessing. There are rare exceptions set by Church law.',
 'CCC 1398–1401; Code of Canon Law 844'),
('Corpus Christi', 'What is Corpus Christi?',
 'Corpus Christi, the Solemnity of the Most Holy Body and Blood of Christ, honours Jesus’ presence in the Eucharist. Many parishes hold a Eucharistic procession through the streets on this day.',
 'CCC 1378–1381'),
('Creation', 'Why did God create the world?',
 'God created the world out of love, not out of need, to show forth and share his glory, goodness and happiness. Everything he made is good, and he created human beings in his image, to know and love him.',
 'CCC 293–294, 299; Genesis 1:31'),
('Death', 'Why do Catholics hold a funeral Mass?',
 'At a funeral Mass the Church commends the dead person to God’s mercy, prays for their purification, and comforts the family with the hope of the resurrection. Christ’s victory over death gives Christian death a positive meaning.',
 'CCC 1680–1690; John 11:25'),
('Forgiveness', 'Is there any sin God cannot forgive?',
 'There is no sin, however serious, that God will not forgive to anyone who truly repents. The “sin against the Holy Spirit” is a deliberate refusal to accept his mercy; it is unforgivable only because the person rejects forgiveness.',
 'CCC 982, 1864; Matthew 12:31'),
('Gossip', 'Is gossip a sin?',
 'Yes. Detraction (revealing someone’s faults without a valid reason) and calumny (spreading lies about them) harm a person’s reputation, which everyone has a right to. They offend against the eighth commandment, and harm done should be repaired.',
 'CCC 2477–2479, 2487'),
('Holy Family', 'Who is the Holy Family?',
 'Jesus, Mary and Joseph. Their home in Nazareth is the model for every Christian family, a “domestic church” of prayer, work and love. The Feast of the Holy Family is celebrated in the Christmas season.',
 'CCC 531–534, 1655–1658; Luke 2:51–52'),
('Hope', 'What is Christian hope?',
 'Hope is the theological virtue by which we desire heaven and eternal life as our happiness, trusting in Christ’s promises and relying on the help of the Holy Spirit, not on our own strength.',
 'CCC 1817–1821; Romans 5:5'),
('Lectio Divina', 'What is Lectio Divina?',
 'Lectio Divina (“divine reading”) is a slow, prayerful reading of Scripture in four steps: read a passage, meditate on it, respond in prayer, and rest in God in contemplation. It is an ancient way to let God speak to us through his Word.',
 'CCC 1177, 2708'),
('Lent', 'Why do Catholics give something up for Lent?',
 'Giving up something we enjoy is a small act of penance that unites us with Christ’s forty days of fasting, trains us in self-control and makes room for God and for the poor. It is best joined to more prayer and generosity.',
 'CCC 1434–1439; Matthew 6:16–18'),
('Mary', 'What does it mean to consecrate oneself to Jesus through Mary?',
 'It means entrusting oneself completely to Jesus through Mary, the surest way to him, as taught by Saint Louis de Montfort. Saint John Paul II took the motto “Totus Tuus” (“Totally yours”) from this devotion.',
 'CCC 2677–2679; John 19:27'),
('Mass', 'What should I wear to Mass?',
 'There is no uniform, but our clothing should show respect for the Lord we meet. Modest, tidy clothes, according to local custom, help us and others to pray. What matters most is a heart prepared to worship.',
 'CCC 1387'),
('Mercy', 'What is the Year of Mercy or a Jubilee?',
 'A Jubilee or Holy Year is a special year of grace proclaimed by the Pope, usually every 25 years, with pilgrimages, the opening of Holy Doors and opportunities for forgiveness and renewal. Pope Francis proclaimed an extraordinary Jubilee of Mercy in 2015–2016, and an ordinary Jubilee of Hope in 2025.',
 'Leviticus 25:10'),
('Pilgrimage', 'Why do Catholics go on pilgrimage?',
 'A pilgrimage is a prayerful journey to a holy place, such as Jerusalem, Rome, Lourdes or a local shrine. It expresses our life as a journey to God, and is a time for prayer, penance and conversion.',
 'CCC 2691; Psalm 122'),
('Precepts', 'What are the precepts of the Church?',
 'They are minimum duties for Catholics: attend Mass on Sundays and holy days; confess serious sins at least once a year; receive Communion at least during the Easter season; observe the days of fasting and abstinence; and help provide for the needs of the Church.',
 'CCC 2041–2043'),
('Rapture', 'Do Catholics believe in the “rapture”?',
 'The Church does not teach a secret rapture before the end. Catholics believe that Christ will come again visibly in glory to judge the living and the dead, and that the faithful will be caught up to meet him at that one Second Coming.',
 'CCC 668–682; 1 Thessalonians 4:16–17'),
('Religious Life', 'What are religious brothers and sisters?',
 'They are men and women who consecrate their lives to God by the vows of poverty, chastity and obedience, usually living in community under a rule, such as Benedictines, Franciscans or Carmelites. They serve through prayer, teaching, nursing, mission and more.',
 'CCC 914–933'),
('Sabbath', 'How should Catholics keep Sunday holy?',
 'By attending Mass, and by resting from unnecessary work and business, making time for God, family, rest, works of mercy and the care of the sick and elderly.',
 'CCC 2184–2188'),
('Sacred Heart', 'What is devotion to the Sacred Heart of Jesus?',
 'It honours the heart of Jesus as the symbol of his boundless love for us. Jesus revealed his Heart to Saint Margaret Mary Alacoque in the 1670s and asked for acts of reparation, such as Holy Communion on the first Friday of each month.',
 'CCC 478; John 19:34'),
('Suffering', 'Why does God allow suffering?',
 'This is a mystery, and no quick answer is enough. God does not cause evil, but permits it and can draw good from it. Jesus took suffering on himself on the cross and gives it meaning. We can unite our sufferings to his, and he promises to wipe away every tear.',
 'CCC 309–314, 1505; Romans 8:28; Revelation 21:4'),
('Tradition', 'What is the difference between Tradition and traditions?',
 'Sacred Tradition (capital T) is the living transmission of the faith from the apostles, which cannot change. Small-t traditions are customs and practices, such as particular devotions or disciplines, which the Church can adapt over time.',
 'CCC 83'),
('Vatican', 'What is the Vatican?',
 'Vatican City is the smallest independent state in the world, inside Rome. It is the home of the Pope and the centre of the Church’s government, the Holy See. Saint Peter’s Basilica is built over the tomb of the apostle Peter.',
 'Lateran Treaty (1929)'),
('Women', 'What is the place of women in the Church?',
 'Women and men are equal in dignity, both made in God’s image. Women have always had indispensable roles: Mary, the Mother of God; the women who first witnessed the Resurrection; foundresses, martyrs, mothers, teachers and four women Doctors of the Church.',
 'CCC 369–372; Mulieris Dignitatem'),
]
